From 7aaf60f105544b70b4cf25f9eb44ace4b00a61fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@openai.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 16:12:43 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Retain adopted Vulkan backing with explicit Metal address
 placement

The wrapped-buffer hook currently chooses every Metal address itself and
leaves the caller to retain Vulkan memory through all command uses. Add
VkBufferDescriptor and an additive adoption method with AddressPlacement,
RangeToken and a backing owner. Preserve the existing method's defaults and
refusal names.

Keep the owner with FixedPages rather than the address token, so an
argument-only resident table retains it after the Metal range is released.
Install it before publication, count its caller destructor with ProgramObject,
and release it outside the memory lock and exit section. Leave it to process
exit when the runtime leaves the Vulkan objects there too. Document that
overlapping adopted buffers do not establish alias hazard identity.

Three real Vulkan adoption tests pass on lavapipe with synchronization
validation: placement/refusal owners, pending named/uncommitted work, and an
argument-only compute dispatch whose backing outlives its address. The existing
private-wrapper regression and both compiler/AIR public-surface tables pass.
Runtime Clippy and rustdoc pass with warnings denied. The selected legacy
shared-wrapper test stops later at an owned texture fixture rejected by NDK
LLVM 21; its manual teardown is skipped on that panic. No fixture or validation
suppression is included.
