 - `Device::new_buffer_with_vk_buffer` makes a buffer over the consumer's `VkBuffer` and memory,
   such as an imported dma-buf, made with `Device::vk_buffer_usage()`. The runtime never frees it.
+  `new_buffer_with_vk_buffer_descriptor(VkBufferDescriptor)` adds explicit `AddressPlacement`,
+  an optional `RangeToken`, and an optional `backing_owner`. The range token drops when the Metal
+  address is free. The backing owner stays until every runtime memory hold is gone, including a
+  pending command's resident table after the Metal buffer itself was released; its destructor
+  may destroy the caller's Vulkan objects. Both are returned by dropping before a refused
+  creation returns. The old call keeps its runtime-picked address and no lifetime hooks.
+  Adoption requires a live, already bound buffer on this same Vulkan device with the stated
+  creation, usage, allocation flags and coherent CPU mapping. It neither imports arbitrary host
+  pages nor gathers discontiguous allocations into one buffer; a pinned Metal address does not
+  move Vulkan memory. The caller still orders external CPU and GPU access against runtime work.
+  Overlapping adopted buffers keep separate hazard identities; sharing a backing owner does not
+  register aliases. The consumer must order accesses through overlapping views explicitly.
 
